## Workflow

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Raw DNA file<br/>23andMe format] --> B[PLINK<br/>--23file]
    B --> C[convertf<br/>to EIGENSTRAT]
    C --> D[mergeit<br/>with AADR]
    D --> E[SNP count report]
    E --> F[Model definition<br/>target, sources, right set]
    F --> G[qpAdm]
    G --> H[Parse coefficients,<br/>SE, p-value]
    H --> I[Run ledger + plot]
```

Every external command writes its stdout and stderr to `work/logs/` and appends a line to
`work/run_ledger.jsonl`. **Failed and rejected models are kept**, not silently dropped.

## What is and isn't implemented

| Implemented in this repository | Not yet validated or implemented |
|---|---|
| PLINK `--23file` conversion | Full build / allele / strand harmonization with per-reason loss reporting |
| `convertf` and `mergeit` merge with AADR | Batch or rotation scheduling of many models |
| SNP counts at each stage | PCA integration |
| Frozen model manifest and deterministic run IDs (SHA-256 of model and dataset hashes) | Matched benchmark against direct ADMIXTOOLS runs |
| Single-model classic qpAdm run | Repeat-run reproducibility tests |
| Output parser with tests (warns instead of guessing) | Parser validation across ADMIXTOOLS versions |
| Run ledger that keeps failed and rejected runs | Deployed Streamlit app audit |
| Descriptive coefficient plot with fit status | |

The validation plan is in the paper ([`docs/`](docs/)). Until it is completed, please do
not describe this pipeline as "fully reproducible".

## Requirements

- Python 3.9+ and `pip install -r requirements.txt`
- [PLINK 1.9](https://www.cog-genomics.org/plink/) on your `PATH`
- [ADMIXTOOLS](https://github.com/DReichLab/AdmixTools) (`convertf`, `mergeit`, `qpAdm`) on your `PATH`
- An AADR release in EIGENSTRAT format (`.geno`, `.snp`, `.ind`) from
  [Harvard Dataverse](https://reich.hms.harvard.edu/allen-ancient-dna-resource-aadr-downloadable-genotypes-present-day-and-ancient-dna-data).
  Record the exact release version you use.

Tool locations can be set with `--plink`, `--convertf`, `--mergeit`, and `--qpadm`.

## Quick start

```bash
git clone https://github.com/AELIUS-22/qpAdm-notebook.git
cd qpAdm-notebook
pip install -r requirements.txt

# 1. Prepare: raw file -> PLINK -> EIGENSTRAT -> merged with AADR
python -m qpadm_pipeline prepare \
    --raw my_raw_data.txt \
    --sample-id MySample \
    --reference-prefix /path/to/aadr/v62.0_1240k_public

# 2. Define a model (copy and edit examples/example_config.json), then run it
python -m qpadm_pipeline run --config examples/example_config.json

# 3. List every run (including rejected and failed ones) and plot
python -m qpadm_pipeline report --out ancestry_plot.png
```

Run the tests with `python -m pytest`.

### Model file

```json
{
  "target": "MySample",
  "sources": ["Onge.DG", "Indus_Periphery_West", "Russia_Sintashta_MLBA"],
  "right": ["Mbuti.DG", "Russia_Ust_Ishim.DG", "Russia_Kostenki14", "Han.DG"],
  "allsnps": true,
  "phase": "exploratory"
}
```

The population labels in `examples/` are **illustrative only**. Check the exact labels in
your AADR `.ind` file. The **right set is part of the statistical hypothesis**, so choose
and justify it yourself. Label each run `benchmark`, `sensitivity` or `exploratory`.

## Output

```
work/
  qc_report.json        SNP counts per stage
  run_ledger.jsonl      every command and every model result
  logs/                 stdout and stderr of every step
  models/<run_id>/      left.txt, right.txt, qpadm.par, manifest.json
  merged/               merged dataset
```

## Interpreting results

- A model with **p > 0.05 is "not rejected" at alpha = 0.05** under that data and right
  set. It is **not** proven correct. Several different models can be compatible.
- A **rejected** model can still print coefficients. Do not present them as a result.
- Coefficients outside 0-1 are flagged as `infeasible_coefficients`.
- The plot is descriptive and does not replace the model-fit test.
- A high p-value does not identify the "true" model. Choosing a model by highest p-value is
  cherry-picking, so report all justified variants.
- Coordinate-space tools (Global25, Vahaduo) are a separate, exploratory evidence track and
  are not comparable to qpAdm p-values.

## Literature benchmark (from the paper, not output of this code)

Published population-level qpAdm results compiled in the paper, shown for orientation. They
are **not** newly generated by this repository and are **not** state-wide averages. Standard
errors were not available in the accessible sources.

| Population (panel) | Region | AASI-related % | Indus Periphery % | Steppe_MLBA % | Model p-value |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Ulladan | Kerala | 61.9 | 35.7 | 2.4 | 0.2386 (not rejected) |
| Palliyar | Tamil Nadu | 64.7 | 33.0 | 2.3 | 0.0337 (rejected) |
| Irula | Tamil Nadu | 57.3 | 40.1 | 2.5 | 0.2489 (not rejected) |
| Lohana | Gujarat | 10.4 | 64.3 | 25.2 | 0.0517 (not rejected) |
| GujaratiA | Gujarat ancestry; diaspora panel | 11.6 | 63.9 | 24.5 | 0.3449 (not rejected) |
| Yadav_Rajasthan | Rajasthan | 15.8 | 61.6 | 22.6 | 0.0765 (not rejected) |
| Brahmin_Haryana | Haryana | 18.2 | 57.2 | 24.6 | 0.4269 (not rejected) |
| Rajput | Haryana | 20.3 | 58.4 | 21.2 | 0.0177 (rejected) |
| Bhumihar_Bihar | Bihar | 20.7 | 52.7 | 26.6 | 0.0514 (not rejected) |
| Yadav_UP | Uttar Pradesh | 17.3 | 61.4 | 21.3 | 0.2081 (not rejected) |
| Bhumihar_UP | Uttar Pradesh | 23.4 | 53.2 | 23.4 | 0.0174 (rejected) |
| Brahmin_UP | Uttar Pradesh | 24.1 | 50.2 | 25.7 | 0.2591 (not rejected) |

The broad pattern is a southern, AASI-shifted group, a western group with more Indus
Periphery-related ancestry, and northern groups with about 21-27% Steppe_MLBA-related
ancestry. Several rows are rejected, which is why coefficients must always be read together
with model fit.

## Limitations

- AASI-related ancestry is a coefficient under a surrogate-source configuration. No
  directly sequenced AASI genome exists.
- qpAdm non-rejection is not proof of a unique history, and weak source proxies or
  right-set choices cannot be repaired by a wrapper.
- Endogamy, founder effects, later migration and sex-biased demography mean that state
  names are descriptive geography, not homogeneous genetic units.
- Y-chromosome haplogroups cannot be inferred from autosomal population summaries.
- Sparse consumer arrays can give low overlap with the 1240K panel. Check
  `qc_report.json` first.
- Genetic ancestry is not a hierarchy of cultural legitimacy. Genetic, archaeological,
  linguistic and oral evidence answer different questions.

## Privacy

**Never commit raw DNA files, PLINK/EIGENSTRAT files, or the `work/` folder.** The
`.gitignore` excludes common patterns, but check before every push. Use only data you have
permission to analyze.

